  "applicability_scope": [
    "User-reported Level 1 review of all eight current Results/Overview.lean theorem statements and every mathematical definition required to interpret their hypotheses and conclusions, recursively through project-local defining dependencies.",
    "Theorem 1.1: Two classes have the stated nonuniform/uniform generation guarantees with and without samples, but their union is not generatable in the repetition-free sample model.",
    "Theorem 1.2: Uniform and nonuniform noisy generation are respectively equivalent to uniform and nonuniform generation without samples, for a UUS class over the canonical universe.",
    "Theorem 1.3: Generation without samples is equivalent to a nondecreasing cover by classes with infinite common intersections.",
    "Theorem 1.4: The same uniform or nonuniform generator remains correct under arbitrary infinite omissions.",
    "Theorem 1.5: For each finite budget, one UUS class simultaneously realizes both adjacent omission and adjacent noise separations.",
    "Theorem 1.6: One class is generatable for every known finite noise bound, with a generator allowed to depend on the bound, but not under an unknown finite amount of noise.",
    "Theorem 1.7: One class is generatable with a membership query on every round, but not without feedback or with any fixed finite total query budget.",
    "Theorem 1.8: Every explicitly indexed countable language family over the naturals is nonuniformly identifiable with truthful membership feedback; equality is of languages, not necessarily their indices.",
    "Definitions 2.1--2.4, 2.8--2.11, 4.1--4.3, 4.8--4.10, 4.13--4.14, 5.1--5.2, 6.1--6.2, 6.5--6.6, and 6.8--6.9 as used in the reviewed statements, plus UUS, the infinite-core cover, truthful response execution, and relevant shared finite-history/contamination interfaces.",
    "Input-relative freshness; no-sample injectivity; known noise bounds count distinct extraneous inputs; finite feedback has a uniform total budget on compatible executions; unrestricted feedback makes one query per round.",
    "Existing semantic qualifications, universe specializations, source assessments, and proof-repair notes remain in force. The statements do not imply machine-level computability or runtime guarantees."
  ],
